\section*{Bose-Einstein Condensation in Continuum Models}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The linked manuscripts present the following claims by OpenAI: For fixed bounded repulsive finite-range potentials, the constant-mode condensate fraction is claimed uniformly positive at sufficiently small density and temperatures up to the density squared; For sufficiently small fixed hard-sphere density, a positive volume-independent temperature is claimed to give a positive thermodynamic constant-mode condensate fraction; Every ground state of a sufficiently dilute fixed-density three-dimensional hard-sphere gas is claimed to place a uniform positive particle fraction in the constant orbital; The hard-sphere ground state is claimed to have the full scaled Bogoliubov momentum law, with depletion $\frac{8}{3\sqrt\pi}\sqrt{\rho a^3}+o(\sqrt{\rho a^3})$; The same leading depletion coefficient is claimed for every fixed bounded nonnegative radial finite-range potential of positive scattering length, uniformly over ground-state density matrices. Together they provide the following support within the scopes described below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Energy and free-energy estimates control cell occupancy and isolation. A short imaginary-time slab supplies joint trajectory bounds; defective-cell handling and insertion comparisons connect distant cells and preserve a density-uniform coherence lower bound. Scaled kinetic and particle-count bounds localize the canonical state. Thermal-slab trajectory estimates permit moving paths between adjacent cells; environment exposure retains movable groups and turns their long-range coherence into the condensate lower bound.

Energy and spatial bounds produce stationary hard-core path samples. Neighbor-cell moves, retained movable groups, and routes through sparse obstacles compare phase coherence across the volume, giving a constant-mode lower bound also for complex ground states. Local energy estimates keep excitations instead of discarding them. One-body covariance and short-trajectory certificates are compared through smoothed deletion measures, while random encounter routes connect local momentum data to the thermodynamic occupation measure before the dilute limit.

Local density tails and ground-state path bounds control occupations at the relevant scattering scale. Spatial comparison transports these local estimates to the constant orbital, identifying every thermodynamic accumulation value before density tends to zero.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
For every fixed bounded repulsive radial finite-range potential, the theorem establishes constant-mode condensation at low fixed density and 0\(\leq\)T\(\leq\)rho\({}^{2}\) in the thermodynamic limit. This is a precise continuum subclass, not a pointwise kernel/ODLRO theorem for all continuum models. The theorem gives a positive constant-orbital occupation fraction for the exact canonical three-dimensional hard-sphere gas at fixed sufficiently small density and one fixed positive temperature. The broad problem statement leaves interaction and ensemble unspecified, and its pointwise long-distance kernel formulation is not itself the theorem. The theorem gives condensation uniformly in the dilute parameter for every hard-sphere ground state and thermodynamic sequence. It concerns T=0 only, so it does not establish the intended positive-temperature continuum Gibbs-state target. Taking the constant test function in the depletion law gives a small depleted fraction and hence condensation in ground states of the dilute fixed hard-sphere model. The positive-temperature continuum-condensation question requires Gibbs states at T>0, which the manuscript does not treat. The depletion law implies ground-state condensation for each fixed bounded nonzero repulsive finite-range radial potential at sufficiently small density. It gives no fixed positive-temperature Gibbs-state assertion in the intended continuum-condensation formulation. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 267, the selected statement is a density-uniform hard-sphere ground-state condensate lower bound for pure and mixed ground states. Positive-temperature condensation and quantum-depletion asymptotics are beyond it. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{HardSphere.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

